\section*{Finite assembly of the height-three chromatic overlap}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
At prime three and height three, the overlap is claimed to lie outside the ordinary thick subcategory generated by lower local spheres, even allowing arbitrary finite cofibers and retracts of derived-complete sphere modules. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 318, 2026-09-25): Integral connected and etale markings retain an exceptional norm-character line on the height-two stratum. Full principal-unit action separates that line from every periodicity power carried by the permitted inputs. Comparisons through ordinary finite-stage coefficients and actual homotopy preserve the character across the whole deformation, giving a representation obstruction to any finite assembly.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 318, 2026-09-25): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed counterexample to that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 318 has no Lean scope page in this snapshot; no formalization of these manuscript results is listed.

